- Discrete Mathematics & Theoretical Computer Science, March 1, 2023, vol. 25:1 - https://doi.org/10.46298/dmtcs.9914\n\nZero-sum partitions of Abelian groups of order $2^n$Article\n\nAuthors: Sylwia Cichacz ; Karol Suchan\n\nThe following problem has been known since the 80's. Let $\\Gamma$ be an Abelian group of order $m$ (denoted $|\\Gamma|=m$), and let $t$ and $m_i$, $1 \\leq i \\leq t$, be positive integers such that $\\sum_{i=1}^t m_i=m-1$.\nDetermine when $\\Gamma^*=\\Gamma\\setminus\\{0\\}$, the set of non-zero elements of $\\Gamma$, can be partitioned into disjoint subsets $S_i$, $1 \\leq i \\leq t$, such that $|S_i|=m_i$ and $\\sum_{s\\in S_i}s=0$ for every $i$, $1 \\leq i \\leq t$. It is easy to check that $m_i\\geq 2$ (for every $i$, $1 \\leq i \\leq t$) and $|I(\\Gamma)|\\neq 1$ are necessary conditions for the existence of such partitions, where $I(\\Gamma)$ is the set of involutions of $\\Gamma$. It was proved that the condition $m_i\\geq 2$ is sufficient if and only if $|I(\\Gamma)|\\in\\{0,3\\}$. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDisjoint zero-sum subsets in Abelian groups and its application -- survey (https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.22245)\nciteturn10academia13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.0 years ago; Authors: Sylwia Cichacz ... We provide a summary of research on disjoint zero-sum subsets in finite Abelian groups, which is a branch of additive group theory and combinatorial number theory. ... Using the idea of disjoint-zero sum subset we provide a solution of this conjecture for $k=3$ and $|\\Gamma|\\cong 4\\pmod{24}$.\nTitle: Disjoint zero-sum subsets in Abelian groups and its application -- survey\nAuthors: Sylwia Cichacz\nDate: Tue Oct 29 17:08:30 2024\n\nWe provide a summary of research on disjoint zero-sum subsets in finite Abelian groups, which is a branch of additive group theory and combinatorial number theory. An orthomorphism of a group $\\Gamma$ is defined as a bijection $\\varphi$ $\\Gamma$ such that the mapping $g \\mapsto g^{-1}\\varphi(g)$ is also bijective. In 1981, Friedlander, Gordon, and Tannenbaum conjectured that when $\\Gamma$ is Abelian, for any $k \\geq 2$ dividing $|\\Gamma| -1$, there exists an orthomorphism of $\\Gamma$ fixing the identity and permuting the remaining elements as products of disjoint $k$-cycles. Using the idea of disjoint-zero sum subset we provide a solution of this conjecture for $k=3$ and $|\\Gamma|\\cong 4\\pmod{24}$. We also present some applications of zero-sum sets in graph labeling.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA random Hall-Paige conjecture | Inventiones mathematicae | Springer Nature Link (https://doi.org/10.1007/s00222-025-01328-x)\nciteturn10search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.7 years ago; Crawled: 3 weeks ago; Given a sequence a_{1}, a_{2},\\ldots , a_{k}$a_{1}, a_{2},\\ldots , a_{k}$ (a_{i}\\geq 2$a_{i}\\geq 2$) with \\sum a_{i}=n-1$\\sum a_{i}=n-1$, where n=|G|$n=|G|$, when can we partition the non-identity elements of an abelian group into zero-sum sets of size a_{1}, a_{2},\\ldots , a_{k}$a_{1}, a_{2},\\ldots , a_{k}$? ... Let \\ell $\\ell $ denote the number of a_{i}$a_{i}$ such that a_{i}=2$a_{i}=2$. ... In particular, our characterisation confirms the following conjecture of Cichacz [16].\n\n### Conjecture 1.6\nDespite the apparent lack of structure in the problem as evidenced by a rich family of counterexamples, in Sect. 6.3, we give a complete characterisation of the (large) abelian groups and integer sequences for which the desired partition exists. Various special cases of this problem were investigated by several authors, for example see [17] and all the references therein. In particular, our characterisation confirms the following conjecture of Cichacz [16].\n\n### Conjecture 1.6\n\nCichacz, [16]\n\nLet G$G$ be an abelian group with at least 3 involutions and suppose we have numbers r_{1}, \\dots , r_{t}\\geq 3$r_{1}, \\dots , r_{t}\\geq 3$ with r_{1}+\\cdots +r_{t}=|G|-1$r_{1}+\\cdots +r_{t}=|G|-1$. Then there is a partition of G\\setminus {e}=Z_{1}\\cup \\cdots \\cup Z_{t}$G\\setminus {e}=Z_{1}\\cup \\cdots \\cup Z_{t}$ where each Z_{i}$Z_{i}$ is a zero-sum set of size r_{i}$r_{i}$.\n\n#### 1.1.4 Harmonious groups\n\nGiven a group G$G$, not necessarily abelian, a harmonious ordering is an ordering of the elements of G$G$ as a_{1},a_{2},\\ldots , a_{n}$a_{1},a_{2},\\ldots , a_{n}$ such that a_{1}a_{2}, a_{2}a_{3}, \\ldots , a_{n-1}a_{n}, a_{n} a_{1}$a_{1}a_{2}, a_{2}a_{3}, \\ldots , a_{n-1}a_{n}, a_{n} a_{1}$ is also an ordering of the elements of G$G$ (i.e. the latter sequence contains no repetitions). Groups which have harmonious orderings are called harmonious. Harmonious groups were first introduced in 1991 by Beals, Gallian, Headley, and Jungreis [10]. They gave a characterisation of all abelian harmonious groups. Evans asked for a complete characterisation.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nZero-sum partitions of Abelian groups of order $2^n$ (https://arxiv.org/abs/2111.05394)\nciteturn10academia14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5.0 years ago; Title: Zero-sum partitions of Abelian groups of order $2^n$\nAuthors: Sylwia Cichacz, Karol Suchan ... For other groups (i.e., for which $|I(\\Gamma)|\\neq 3$ and $|I(\\Gamma)|>1$), only the case of any group $\\Gamma$ with $\\Gamma\\cong(Z_2)^n$ for some positive integer $n$ has been analyzed completely so far, and it was shown independently by several authors that $m_i\\geq 3$ is sufficient in this case.\nTitle: Zero-sum partitions of Abelian groups of order $2^n$\nAuthors: Sylwia Cichacz, Karol Suchan\nDate: Tue Nov  9 20:04:46 2021\n\nThe following problem has been known since the 80's. Let $\\Gamma$ be an Abelian group of order $m$ (denoted $|\\Gamma|=m$), and let $t$ and $m_i$, $1 \\leq i \\leq t$, be positive integers such that $\\sum_{i=1}^t m_i=m-1$. Determine when $\\Gamma^*=\\Gamma\\setminus\\{0\\}$, the set of non-zero elements of $\\Gamma$, can be partitioned into disjoint subsets $S_i$, $1 \\leq i \\leq t$, such that $|S_i|=m_i$ and $\\sum_{s\\in S_i}s=0$ for every $i$, $1 \\leq i \\leq t$. It is easy to check that $m_i\\geq 2$ (for every $i$, $1 \\leq i \\leq t$) and $|I(\\Gamma)|\\neq 1$ are necessary conditions for the existence of such partitions, where $I(\\Gamma)$ is the set of involutions of $\\Gamma$. It was proved that the condition $m_i\\geq 2$ is sufficient if and only if $|I(\\Gamma)|\\in\\{0,3\\}$. For other groups (i.e., for which $|I(\\Gamma)|\\neq 3$ and $|I(\\Gamma)|>1$), only the case of any group $\\Gamma$ with $\\Gamma\\cong(Z_2)^n$ for some positive integer $n$ has been analyzed completely so far, and it was shown independently by several authors that $m_i\\geq 3$ is sufficient in this case. Moreover, recently Cichacz and Tuza proved that, if $|\\Gamma|$ is large enough and $|I(\\Gamma)|>1$, then $m_i\\geq 4$ is sufficient. In this paper we generalize this result for every Abelian group of order $2^n$. Namely, we show that the condition $m_i\\geq 3$ is sufficient for $\\Gamma$ such that $|I(\\Gamma)|>1$ and $|\\Gamma|=2^n$, for every positive integer $n$. We also present some applications of this result to graph magic- and anti-magic-type labelings.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAlso available at http://amc-journal.eu (https://dlib.si/stream/URN%3ANBN%3ASI%3ADOC-85TP6JEE/8d3df9a9-8613-42b4-b64e-c832063ff61e/PDF)\nciteturn10search15 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8 months ago; Cichacz: On zero sum-partition of Abelian groups into three sets and group distance. . . ... Theorem 3.2. ... Γ has the CSP(3) -property if\nS. Cichacz: On zero sum-partition of Abelian groups into three sets and group distance. . .\n421\nProof. For ni = nj = 1 we have that P\na∈Ai a ̸= P\na∈Aj a. For ni = 2, it is easy to see\nP\na∈Ai a ̸= g0.\nLet ι0 = g0. Recall that since I = {ι0, ι1, . . . , ι2k−1} is a subgr